[section:concepts Concepts]

This chapter describes the named requirements and concepts that make up
the public interface of Boost.Taar. Each concept is given a semantic
description here; the formal C++ definitions live in the Reference
chapter and can be reached from the entries below.

* [link boost_taar.concepts.matcher Matcher]: selects whether a request
  belongs to a handler.
* [link boost_taar.concepts.arg_provider Argument Provider]: produces
  one argument for a `rest` handler from the request.
* [link boost_taar.concepts.response_from response_from]: converts a
  handler's return value into an HTTP response.
* [link boost_taar.concepts.rest_arg_cast rest_arg_cast]: converts an
  argument provider's output into the type a handler expects.
* [link boost_taar.concepts.context_aware Context-aware handler]: a
  handler that receives the contexts in scope.
* [link boost_taar.concepts.observer Registration observer]: a callable
  told about every route as it is registered.

[section:matcher Matcher]

A /Matcher/ is a callable that decides whether a given HTTP request belongs
to a particular handler. Matchers are obtained by composing the matcher
objects exported by `boost::taar::matcher` (`method`, `target`, `header`,
`cookie`, `version`) with the relational and boolean operators defined in
`<boost/taar/matcher/operand.hpp>`.

[heading Required expressions]

A type is a /Matcher/ when a `matcher::operand` can be constructed from it.
Besides the composed DSL expressions, that includes any callable returning
`bool` that takes a `boost::beast::http::request_header<> const&` and a
`matcher::context&`, optionally followed by a `boost::urls::url_view
const&` (the parsed target), a `cookies const&` (the parsed `Cookie`
headers), or both in either order. The session parses the target and the
cookies only when some registered matcher asks for them.

[table
[[Expression][Semantics]]
[[`m(req, ctx)`][Returns `bool`. May write to `ctx.path_args` and other
 context state; the context is cleared before each matcher runs.]]
[[`m(req, ctx, url)`][As above, with the request target parsed as a URL.]]
[[`m(req, ctx, cookies)`][As above, with the parsed cookies.]]
]

[heading Metadata]

A composed matcher is a `matcher::operand<Callable, Metadata>`. `Metadata`
is a type-level AST (`and_`, `or_`, `not_` over leaf tags such as
`method_eq_constraint` and `target_constraint`), and `captures()` returns
the values each leaf captured, in leaf order. Generators inspect the AST
at compile time to decide whether they can express a route, and read the
captures at run time. A plain callable used as a matcher has
`any_metadata` and cannot be documented.

[endsect]

[section:arg_provider Argument Provider]

An /Argument Provider/ is an object that, given an HTTP request, produces
exactly one argument for a wrapped callable. Argument providers are
supplied as the trailing arguments to `taar::handler::rest`.

The library exports `path_arg`, `query_arg`, `query_list_arg` (every
occurrence of a query key, `?tag=a&tag=b`, into a container),
`header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, `string_body_arg`, `json_body_arg`,
`url_encoded_form_data_arg`, `multipart_form_arg`, `stream_body_arg`,
`multipart_stream_arg`, `target_arg` and `body_size_arg`, plus
`with_default` to substitute a value when a provider reports
`argument_not_found` and `constrained` to validate the value. User code
can define new argument providers by satisfying the required expressions.

Providers report through `taar::result<V>` (that is,
`boost::system::result<V, taar::failure>`). The not-found signal is
`taar::failure{taar::error::argument_not_found}`, with no message: it is
the one failure that `with_default` and `std::optional` parameters absorb.
Any other failure should carry a code that says what went wrong and a
message that reads on its own, such as
`taar::failure{taar::error::argument_ambiguous, "header 'x-id' occurs more than once"}`;
the binder nests it as the cause of an `invalid_argument_format` failure
naming the argument and its position (see
[link boost_taar.design.errors Error mapping per surface]).

[heading Required expressions]

Given a provider `p`, a request `req` whose body type the provider
declares, and a `matcher::context` `ctx`:

[table
[[Expression][Semantics]]
[[`p(req, ctx)`][Returns `taar::result<V>`. The value is passed
 through `rest_arg_cast` to the callable's parameter type. A failure
 `f` with `f.is(taar::error::argument_not_found)` means the argument is
 absent; any other failure is thrown as a `taar::exception` and reaches
 the soft error handler (or the JSON-RPC mapper, or the MCP tool error
 handler). A provider returning `boost::system::result<V>` with a plain
 `error_code` is still accepted; its code becomes a failure without a
 message.]]
[[`p.name()`][The argument's name, used in diagnostics and documentation.]]
[[`p.description()`][Optional. A human-readable description used by
 documentation generators. Named providers take it as a trailing
 constructor string; body providers take `handler::description{...}`.]]
]

The JSON-RPC and MCP layers have their own providers (`rpc_arg`,
`mcp_arg`, `uri_arg`) with the same shape but a different first
argument: the decoded call rather than the HTTP request.

[endsect]

[section:response_from response_from]

Any user-defined return type can be converted to an HTTP response by
overloading the `boost::taar::response_from_tag` tag (declared in
`<boost/taar/core/response_from_tag.hpp>`). This extension point is the
basis for the automatic JSON conversion of `boost::json::value`,
`boost::json::object` and `boost::json::array`, the text conversion of
strings and arithmetic types, and the file conversion used by
`taar::handler::htdocs`.

[endsect]

[section:rest_arg_cast rest_arg_cast]

`rest_arg_cast` is the extension point used by argument providers to turn a
string view (or JSON value) extracted from the request into the typed
argument of a callable. Customise it by overloading
`boost::taar::handler::rest_arg_cast_tag` for your type. Types that
`boost::json::value_to` understands, including `boost::describe`d
structs, work without any overload.

[endsect]

[section:context_aware Context-aware handler]

A /Context-aware handler/ opts in to receiving the contexts the session
has in scope. The handlers produced by `handler::rest`, `with_session`,
`jsonrpc::endpoint::handler()` and `mcp::endpoint::handler()` all satisfy
it.

[table
[[Expression][Semantics]]
[[`H::context_aware`][`static constexpr bool`, `true`.]]
[[`typename H::request_type`][The `http::request<Body>` the handler reads;
 the session parses the body accordingly.]]
[[`h(ctxs, req, ctx)`][`ctxs` is a `handler::contexts<C...>`, a tuple of
 `shared_ptr<C>`; `req` is `request_type const&`; `ctx` is
 `matcher::context const&`. Returns a response, an awaitable of one, or a
 streaming generator.]]
]

Handlers that are not context-aware keep the classic
`(request, matcher::context)` signature. Wrappers such as `with_session`
extend `ctxs` with their own object before calling the inner handler, so
the set of types in scope grows outward-in.

[endsect]

[section:observer Registration observer]

A /Registration observer/ is any callable accepting
`(matcher::operand const&, Handler const&)`, passed as a trailing
argument to `register_request_handler`. It runs once, before the route is
installed, and may inspect the operand's metadata and captures and the
handler's `params()` and `callable_result_type` when present.
`openapi::generator::describe(...)` returns one.

[endsect]

[endsect]
